(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-medical-imaging-quiz"]={"title":"Principles of Medical Imaging Quiz 1 — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What equipment does fluoroscopy require?","choices":["A superconducting magnet requiring dedicated site construction beforehand and high ongoing cost","A handheld transducer connected to a portable processing unit, needing neither a tilting table nor a shielded examination room","An x-ray unit fitted for controlled motion of the source, the imaging sensor and the patient, with a tilting table","A gamma camera mounted on a rotating gantry that circles the patient, with a fixed table and a detector housing that does not move"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the tube moves freely back and forth to image the patient. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Why does the deck name cancer as a risk factor for all contrast material?","choices":["Because the dyes provoke chronic inflammation, and chronic inflammation is carcinogenic","Because the dyes are technically radioactive, as are the imaging techniques themselves","Because the dyes are nephrotoxic, and chronic renal injury raises malignancy risk over time","Because the dyes are retained in tissue indefinitely, and retained foreign material transforms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck states the risk in exactly those terms. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is a vasculogram?","choices":["Not one specific test — any of the modalities can be used to image the vessels","A specific x-ray study in which iodine is injected directly into a peripheral vein","A specific nuclear study in which technetium is used to map perfusion to an organ","A specific ultrasound study in which the transducer is placed within the vessel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — x-ray, ultrasound, computed tomography and magnetic resonance all can. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which substance is assigned a Hounsfield number of zero by convention?","choices":["Fat","Water","Air","Bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — other tissues take a range of Hounsfield numbers according to their density. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How is a conventional radiograph produced?","choices":["Ionizing radiation and light strike a photosensitive surface, producing a latent image that is then processed to become visible","High-frequency sound waves bounce off the tissue and return to a transducer, which then builds up the whole image","A magnetic field aligns hydrogen atoms, which emit radio waves when the field is released","A gamma camera detects photons emitted by an injected radioisotope within the patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it requires a source, a method to record the image, and a way to process it. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What are the named indications for intravenous contrast in computed tomography?","choices":["Inflammation, cancer staging, tumour delineation, vasculopathy, emboli, thrombi, stenosis and aneurysms","Fracture, dislocation, foreign body, joint effusion, bone tumour, osteomyelitis and arthritis","Stroke, demyelination, cord compression, disc herniation, meningitis and hydrocephalus","Pneumonia, effusion, pneumothorax, atelectasis, emphysema and interstitial lung disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the vascular indications and the oncological ones are listed together. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the window in computed tomography?","choices":["The range of Hounsfield numbers pre-selected for display, spread over the available grey scale","The physical aperture of the gantry through which the patient passes","The interval during which the contrast sits at its peak concentration in the vessel","The slice thickness selected before the scan is acquired"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — for example only those densities between −100 and +300. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does a positron emission tomography scanner use in place of an x-ray tube?","choices":["A rotating fan beam, recording transmission through the patient at thousands of points","A transducer, recording high-frequency sound waves reflected back from the tissue","A varying magnetic field, recording radio waves emitted as hydrogen atoms realign","A gamma camera, recording emissions from an injected dye in two dimensions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the radiation comes from the patient, not from the machine. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What defines a conventional radiograph as distinct from other x-ray studies?","choices":["It uses ionizing radiation together with an intravenous iodinated contrast agent","It uses no ionizing radiation at all and relies on acoustic properties instead","It uses a rotating fan beam and computer reconstruction into cross-sections","It uses ionizing radiation without any added contrast material such as barium or iodine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — adding contrast makes it a different study. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What are the five basic radiographic densities, ordered from whitest to blackest?","choices":["Metal, fluid (soft tissue), calcium (bone), air, fat","Metal, calcium (bone), fluid (soft tissue), fat, air","Air, fat, fluid (soft tissue), calcium (bone), metal","Calcium, metal, fat, fluid (soft tissue), air"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — metal usually absorbs all x-rays and air absorbs the least. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"In which patients is ultrasound named as often the first study of choice?","choices":["Oncology and post-operative patients, and it is also used to stage known disease","Female pelvis and paediatric patients, and it is also used to guide procedures","Adult chest and geriatric patients, and it is also used to screen for fractures","Head-injured and intoxicated patients, and it is also used to exclude a bleed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — no radiation is the reason it leads in both groups. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"On the deck's labelled pelvic radiograph, how are bone, gas and the prosthesis described?","choices":["Bone is radiopaque, gas is radiolucent, and the metal prosthesis is very opaque","Bone is radiolucent, gas is radiopaque, and the metal prosthesis is very lucent","Bone is very opaque, gas is radiopaque, and the metal prosthesis is radiolucent","Bone is radiopaque, gas is very opaque, and the metal prosthesis is radiolucent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — one image showing three of the five densities at once. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How is radiation dose defined and expressed?","choices":["Ionizing energy absorbed per unit of volume, in sieverts only, where one sievert is one joule per litre","Ionizing energy delivered per exposure, in millisieverts only, where one millisievert is one milliwatt","Ionizing energy absorbed per unit of mass, in grays or milligrays, where one gray is one joule per kilogram","Ionizing energy emitted per unit of time, in grays or milligrays, where one gray is one joule per second"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and equivalent dose is then expressed in sieverts or millisieverts. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the stated trade-off of an open magnetic resonance scanner?","choices":["Decreased quality of imaging, which is the price paid for the open configuration","Increased scan duration, which is the price paid for the open configuration","Increased radiation dose, which is the price paid for the open configuration","Decreased soft tissue contrast only, with spatial resolution otherwise preserved"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is available, but the deck flags the loss of quality. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which agent is used for magnetic resonance contrast, and what are its indications?","choices":["Barium, for luminal structures anywhere from the upper oesophagus down to the rectum","Gadolinium, for central nervous system tumours, metastases, soft tissue masses and arthrograms","Iohexol, for central nervous system tumours, metastases, soft tissue masses and arthrograms","Technetium-99, for bone scans, myocardial perfusion and functional brain imaging"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and central nervous system tumours are typically characterised without biopsy. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is given alongside intravenous iodinated contrast to protect the kidneys?","choices":["One gram of acetylcysteine","One dose of intravenous furosemide","One litre of normal saline","One litre of lactated Ringer's"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the only volume the deck specifies, and it is renal protection. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the stated safety record of technetium-99?","choices":["Allergic reactions are rare, though documented nephrotoxicity requires pre-hydration","Allergic reactions are rare, though documented hepatotoxicity requires liver monitoring","Allergic reactions are rare, and no organ damage has previously been documented","Allergic reactions are common, though no organ damage has previously been documented"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — neither nephrotoxicity nor hepatotoxicity is documented. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"On a T1-weighted image, how does tissue with low water content appear?","choices":["Bright, which is the reverse of the same tissue on a T2-weighted image","Dark, which is the same as that tissue would appear on a T2-weighted image","Bright, which is the same as that tissue would appear on a T2-weighted image","Dark, which is the reverse of the same tissue on a T2-weighted image"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — on T1, low water is white and high water is dark. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What does the deck call the most important thing to remember about ultrasound orientation?","choices":["Which frequency is selected: high for superficial structures, low for deeper structures","Where the indicator is: on the right of the screen in cardiac imaging, on the left for everything else","Where the indicator is: on the left of the screen in cardiac imaging, on the right for everything else","How deep the focus is set: shallow for superficial structures, deep for abdominal structures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is called crucial, because the whole image flips with it. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which density is described as the most dense naturally occurring material?","choices":["Fluid","Fat","Calcium","Metal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it absorbs most x-rays, though metal absorbs essentially all of them. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How does the organ dose of neonatal abdominal computed tomography compare with the adult study?","choices":["It is twice as high, twenty against ten","It is half as high, ten against twenty","It is the same, since the scanner protocol is identical","It is a tenth as high, since paediatric protocols are dose-reduced"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the smaller the patient, the higher the organ dose. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does the colour Doppler setting add to a standard ultrasound image?","choices":["Metabolic uptake and intensity, on an image otherwise showing only anatomical structure","Direction of flow and velocity, on an image otherwise made of white, grey and black","Depth of penetration and resolution, on an image otherwise limited to superficial planes","Density in Hounsfield units and windowing, on an image otherwise showing five densities"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — colour is the flow overlay, and blood is what it is used for. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are the midsagittal and parasagittal planes?","choices":["Midsagittal runs through the umbilicus; parasagittal sections lie above and below it","Midsagittal runs across the shoulders; parasagittal sections lie anterior and posterior","Midsagittal runs at the level of the heart; parasagittal sections are angled off the axis","Midsagittal runs in the body's midline; parasagittal sections lie to either side of it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the midsagittal plane is also called the median plane. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How is a posterior-anterior chest film oriented when you look at it?","choices":["As if the patient were standing behind you, with their right side on your right","As if you were looking at the patient's feet, with their left side on your right","As if you were looking down from above, with their right side nearest the top","As if the patient were standing in front of you, with their right side on your left"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same convention as facing a patient in the room. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How does the Hounsfield table refine the five basic radiographic densities?","choices":["It separates calcium from metal, which a conventional radiograph cannot tell apart","It separates fat from air, which a conventional radiograph cannot tell apart","It merges bone and soft tissue, which a conventional radiograph shows separately","It separates water from soft tissue, which a conventional radiograph cannot tell apart"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck's asterisk on “Five*” marks exactly that exception. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What should you do if you are unsure which modality to order?","choices":["Order the study with the widest range of densities, and narrow down afterwards","Order the study with the lowest radiation dose, and escalate only if it is negative","Order both the plain film and the cross-sectional study at the same visit","Tell the radiologist what you are looking for, and they can guide the choice"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the question you are asking is the useful thing to communicate. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which enteric pathologies is the abdominal series named as evaluating?","choices":["Cholelithiasis, pancreatitis and hepatic abscess","Peptic ulceration, gastritis and oesophageal stricture","Small bowel obstruction, perforation and volvulus","Appendicitis, diverticulitis and inflammatory bowel disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the three named examples of enteric pathology. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What does the deck say about comparison films?","choices":["They should be displayed only when the radiologist specifically requests them for a read","They must be matched by modality but not by projection, so any old film may be paired","They can be invaluable — old gold — and the old film is displayed adjacent to the matching new one","They are rarely useful once a current study exists, and should be archived rather than displayed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — old posterior-anterior beside new, old lateral beside new. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Who discovered x-rays, and what recognition followed?","choices":["Godfrey Hounsfield, in Britain in 1972, awarded the Nobel Prize for Medicine in 1979","Allan Cormack, in South Africa in 1963, awarded the first Nobel Prize for Physics","Marie Curie, in France in 1898, awarded the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1903","Wilhelm Roentgen, in Germany in 1895, awarded the first Nobel Prize for Physics in 1901"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the technique is still called roentgenography after him. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"In which units is radiation measured?","choices":["Hertz and decibels","Millisieverts and milligrays","Hounsfield units and pixels","Tesla and gauss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — x-ray radiation is low compared with other radiographic modalities. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 9"}]};
